# Fixes a real TypeScript+pnpm symlink-identity issue found while
# verifying this PR: `tsc` (moduleResolution: "bundler") was treating
# @types/react imported via a transitive chain (e.g.
# @react-three/fiber -> its-fine -> @types/react-reconciler) as a
# DIFFERENT module identity than @types/react imported directly by app
# code — even though both resolved to the exact same realpath in the
# pnpm content-addressable store (verified: `realpath` on both paths
# returned an identical file). That produced a batch of spurious
# "cannot be used as a JSX component" errors (Suspense, class-component
# error boundaries, Context.Provider) across files that were typechecking
# clean under a flat npm install moments earlier in the same session —
# a pnpm node_modules-layout artifact, not a real app bug. Publicly
# hoisting these two packages gives every dependency chain the same
# single root-level copy to resolve through, which is the standard fix
# for this known class of issue.
public-hoist-pattern[]=*@types/react*
public-hoist-pattern[]=*@types/react-dom*

# public-hoist-pattern alone didn't fully resolve it — the remaining cause
# is TypeScript's "bundler" moduleResolution treating @types/react reached
# through a deeply nested peer-suffixed chain (e.g.
# @react-three/fiber -> its-fine -> @types/react-reconciler) as a
# different module identity than the one resolved directly by app code,
# even though both point at the identical realpath on disk (verified).
# node-linker=hoisted reproduces the flat, non-symlinked node_modules
# layout a plain npm install already produced (under which this exact
# code verified clean) — it changes only the local install layout, not
# the resolved dependency versions or pnpm-lock.yaml's contents/format.
node-linker=hoisted

